  :tutorial/title "Assignment information"
  :tutorial/content "**MTH 255 — Written Homework 5**\n\nDue: 2026-10-29\nDue time: 11:59 p.m. PDT\nDeadline source: [Fall 2026 Canvas assignment](https://canvas.oregonstate.edu/courses/2064132/assignments/10705976), checked 2026-10-25 (Thursday, October 29).\nDate imported: 2026-10-25\nSource: [MTH255-WHW-Week5_v1.pdf](/api/source?path=%2Fhome%2Fjake%2FDeveloper%2Fstudy%2Fvault%2FF26%2Fsource%2F255%2FWHW-5%2FMTH255-WHW-Week5_v1.pdf)\n\nStudy answers, worked explanations, and corrections discussed in the reveal fields are independently authored additions. The original tasks are retained, including assertions and notation that require qualification.\n\n## Instructions\n\n- Complete all five problems and their labeled subparts. Show clear, neat supporting work or a written explanation where appropriate. Correct answers without supporting work do not earn full credit.\n- Work on the printed template, write/type on the PDF with a tablet, or use your own paper with the same layout.\n- Submit a single PDF to Gradescope under **Written Homework 5**, in the template's six-page format: the cover page followed by one page for each problem. Include the cover and blank problems. If a solution uses multiple physical pages, combine them into one collage page for that problem. A scan of the printed template should preserve its page count and format.\n- The source awards partial credit for logical, organized progress. Canvas states that late homework is not accepted; submit completed work by the deadline.\n- In Problems 1–3, $\\mathbf T$ is the unit tangent in the stated counterclockwise direction and $\\mathbf n$ is the outward unit normal. Preserve the distinction between evaluating a contour integral directly and checking whether Green's theorem applies."
  :db/ensure :tutorial/validate}
 {:db/id "9dbe677d-9a8d-5d73-9cab-fff2ee3533b1"
  :answer/id #uuid "9dbe677d-9a8d-5d73-9cab-fff2ee3533b1"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "The first partial derivatives of $\\mathbf F=\\langle f,g\\rangle$ are $f_x,f_y,g_x,g_y$. Those of $\\mathbf G=\\langle-g,f\\rangle$ are $-g_x,-g_y,f_x,f_y$. Negating or reordering functions preserves continuity. Thus all first partial derivatives of one field are continuous on the region exactly when all first partial derivatives of the other are."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
