 {:db/id "181efbc3-58c9-5d77-9233-3e50034e40ce"
  :question/id #uuid "181efbc3-58c9-5d77-9233-3e50034e40ce"
  :question/problem "**(b)** Show that $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf G$. In particular, show $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=0$ iff $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf G=0$. Conclude that $\\mathbf F$ is source-free iff $\\mathbf G$ is irrotational."
  :question/answer-fields ["b1f05870-a3a6-5713-92fb-c66be8566641"]
  :question/worked-solution "For $\\langle P,Q\\rangle$, divergence is $P_x+Q_y$, while planar curl is $Q_x-P_y$. The minus sign on the first component of $\\mathbf G$ makes its curl equal to the divergence of $\\mathbf F$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "0c023af7-0fa4-5186-8428-fbbabdcb5778"
  :answer/id #uuid "0c023af7-0fa4-5186-8428-fbbabdcb5778"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "Parametrize the counterclockwise curve by $\\mathbf r(t)=(x(t),y(t))$. Where the tangent exists, the outward normal is the clockwise rotation of the tangent, hence $\\mathbf n\\,ds=(y'(t),-x'(t))\\,dt$. Therefore\n\n$$\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=(fy'-gx')\\,dt=(-g,f)\\cdot(x',y')\\,dt=\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.$$\n\nIntegrate this pointwise identity over $C$ (piecewise for a piecewise smooth curve)."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
