 {:db/id "0c4ee11a-932b-509f-9464-bca3b0b4f4af"
  :question/id #uuid "0c4ee11a-932b-509f-9464-bca3b0b4f4af"
  :question/problem "**(c)** Show that\n\n$$\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=\\oint_C\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds.$$\n\n**Source note:** Green's theorem may not apply. Instead, try to show $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=\\mathbf G\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$."
  :question/answer-fields ["e423325b-ccb6-5a16-a938-bf8fae815cba"]
  :question/worked-solution "The equality depends on the orientation and the quarter-turn relating the two fields. It requires the boundary integrals to exist, but does not require either field to be continuously differentiable throughout the interior, so it remains useful when Green's theorem is unavailable."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "2b63134e-55f3-5061-8a0c-a841870a4892"
  :answer/id #uuid "2b63134e-55f3-5061-8a0c-a841870a4892"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "A potential for $\\mathbf G=\\langle-g,f\\rangle$ satisfies $\\zeta_x=-g$ and $\\zeta_y=f$. The definition of a stream function for $\\mathbf F=\\langle f,g\\rangle$ is precisely $\\zeta_y=f$, $\\zeta_x=-g$. The two conditions are identical in both directions."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
