 {:db/id "8391dbe2-9a3e-510f-9e77-9168461afd0d"
  :question/id #uuid "8391dbe2-9a3e-510f-9e77-9168461afd0d"
  :question/problem "**(a)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\langle-y,x\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["a64a5703-da96-51f8-bd37-e4a7f704835d"]
  :question/worked-solution "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. On the unit circle the denominator, when present, equals $1$, so $\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)=\\mathbf r'(t)$. Thus $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,dt=dt$ and the integral is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "ba61bd9f-1b0e-581b-ac75-c6723448df83"
  :answer/id #uuid "ba61bd9f-1b0e-581b-ac75-c6723448df83"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "Here $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=2$. Green's theorem applies because the field is $C^1$ on the entire disk and a neighborhood of it. Therefore\n\n$$\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\iint_D2\\,dA=2\\operatorname{area}(D)=2\\pi.$$"
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
