 {:db/id "83b569b0-0b6b-5273-ae4e-6e0b127fd30d"
  :question/id #uuid "83b569b0-0b6b-5273-ae4e-6e0b127fd30d"
  :question/problem "**(b)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["53586b83-f875-5cd5-a67c-0ff2bafda146"]
  :question/worked-solution "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. On the unit circle the denominator, when present, equals $1$, so $\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)=\\mathbf r'(t)$. Thus $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,dt=dt$ and the integral is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "a7667b11-d5f9-5246-834a-a9f6246331ca"
  :answer/id #uuid "a7667b11-d5f9-5246-834a-a9f6246331ca"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "For $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}>0$, $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=0$. Formally integrating that expression over the punctured disk gives $0$.\n\nThis is not a valid application of Green's theorem to the full disk: the field is undefined at the origin, which lies inside $C$. The actual boundary integral is $2\\pi$."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
