 {:db/id "1702368e-f0bc-528a-a104-7f0612080884"
  :question/id #uuid "1702368e-f0bc-528a-a104-7f0612080884"
  :question/problem "**(b)(iii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ “using” Green's theorem. Address any failure of its hypotheses."
  :question/answer-fields ["8a26eece-73eb-5425-ab5c-008b42b0892b"]
  :question/worked-solution "The formal value $0$ is the result the question asks you to compare, not a correct value of the contour integral. On an annulus $\\varepsilon\\le r\\le1$, Green's theorem has both outer and inner boundaries. The outer integral minus the counterclockwise inner integral is $0$; each counterclockwise integral is $2\\pi$. Omitting the inner boundary loses that contribution."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "77b2897b-cbba-5228-9728-60335f0049bf"
  :answer/id #uuid "77b2897b-cbba-5228-9728-60335f0049bf"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "Direct calculation gives $2\\pi$, while the invalid full-disk use of Green's theorem gives $0$. The domain excludes the origin, so the theorem's regularity hypothesis fails inside $C$. The mismatch comes from ignoring the hole and its inner boundary, rather than from an error in the direct parametrization."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
