 {:db/id "2913247e-6fe4-5e09-8a9d-8346095c1d59"
  :question/id #uuid "2913247e-6fe4-5e09-8a9d-8346095c1d59"
  :question/problem "**(c)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}},\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["07f2f829-22f9-542b-9ade-2169285ea987"]
  :question/worked-solution "Use $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. On the unit circle the denominator, when present, equals $1$, so $\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))=(-\\sin t,\\cos t)=\\mathbf r'(t)$. Thus $\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds=\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,dt=dt$ and the integral is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}1\\,dt=2\\pi$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "d41b273d-8e09-5af7-a5ea-44d7947cab94"
  :answer/id #uuid "d41b273d-8e09-5af7-a5ea-44d7947cab94"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "For $r>0$, $\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=1/r$. The formal area integral can be interpreted as an improper integral:\n\n$$\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\to0^+}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_\\varepsilon^1\\frac1r\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\n=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\to0^+}2\\pi(1-\\varepsilon)=2\\pi.$$\n\nThe standard full-disk theorem does not apply directly because the field is undefined at the origin. The equality can be justified by applying Green's theorem to annuli and taking a limit."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
 {:db/id "b105b52f-d67c-5f04-9f64-f2c5d7a169ce"
