 {:db/id "d13cb879-cfc1-5508-95b7-b45cbb676eab"
  :question/id #uuid "d13cb879-cfc1-5508-95b7-b45cbb676eab"
  :question/problem "**(c)(iii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{-y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}},\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf T\\,ds$ “using” Green's theorem. Address any failure of its hypotheses."
  :question/answer-fields ["b105b52f-d67c-5f04-9f64-f2c5d7a169ce"]
  :question/worked-solution "For a radius-$\\varepsilon$ circle, the counterclockwise circulation (Problem 2) or outward-from-the-small-disk flux (Problem 3) is $2\\pi\\varepsilon$, which tends to zero. The annulus formula is outer integral minus that inner integral $=2\\pi(1-\\varepsilon)$. The Jacobian factor $r$ cancels the singular factor $1/r$ in the improper area integral."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "4e6eb92b-c2bf-5fd9-ac1d-05691eb1508e"
  :answer/id #uuid "4e6eb92b-c2bf-5fd9-ac1d-05691eb1508e"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "Both computations yield $2\\pi$, but the agreement alone does not make a direct full-disk invocation of Green's theorem valid. The origin is excluded from the domain. In this case, annular applications and a limit do justify the equality, because the inner-boundary contribution tends to zero."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
