 {:db/id "dee55dc8-025d-5e2e-81d6-81ca2cb50427"
  :question/id #uuid "dee55dc8-025d-5e2e-81d6-81ca2cb50427"
  :question/problem "**(a)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,y\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["a71b514a-9026-5f64-8d2d-53ce75beba13"]
  :question/worked-solution "On the unit circle, parametrize by $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. The outward unit normal is $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$. Each field in this problem restricts to $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ on $C$, so the flux is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)\\,dt=2\\pi$. Equivalently, rotate the field as in Problem 1(c) and use the corresponding circulation in Problem 2."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "5805a00c-2392-5a80-91e5-b6d845f1b4dc"
  :answer/id #uuid "5805a00c-2392-5a80-91e5-b6d845f1b4dc"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "Here $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=2$. Green's theorem applies because the field is $C^1$ on the entire disk and a neighborhood of it. Therefore\n\n$$\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds=\\iint_D2\\,dA=2\\operatorname{area}(D)=2\\pi.$$"
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
