 {:db/id "08ca2ee1-3c85-5376-b8cd-192882ba0fb1"
  :question/id #uuid "08ca2ee1-3c85-5376-b8cd-192882ba0fb1"
  :question/problem "**(b)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x}{x^2+y^2},\\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["8ba64de1-78df-526b-a684-fb466addd1c0"]
  :question/worked-solution "On the unit circle, parametrize by $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. The outward unit normal is $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$. Each field in this problem restricts to $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ on $C$, so the flux is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)\\,dt=2\\pi$. Equivalently, rotate the field as in Problem 1(c) and use the corresponding circulation in Problem 2."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "d73965e8-b478-5301-a93b-edc40660a598"
  :answer/id #uuid "d73965e8-b478-5301-a93b-edc40660a598"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "For $r=\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}>0$, $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=0$. Formally integrating that expression over the punctured disk gives $0$.\n\nThis is not a valid application of Green's theorem to the full disk: the field is undefined at the origin, which lies inside $C$. The actual boundary integral is $2\\pi$."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
