 {:db/id "928d7776-a536-5089-a550-fc78255e94dc"
  :question/id #uuid "928d7776-a536-5089-a550-fc78255e94dc"
  :question/problem "**(c)(ii)** For $\\mathbf F=\\left\\langle\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}},\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}\\right\\rangle$, calculate $\\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds$ **by hand**. Show your parametrization and work.\n\nIntegral: {{answer}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["52d06fdc-3175-52f3-b479-8c57eb944433"]
  :question/worked-solution "On the unit circle, parametrize by $\\mathbf r(t)=(\\cos t,\\sin t)$, $0\\le t\\le2\\pi$. The outward unit normal is $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ and $ds=dt$. Each field in this problem restricts to $(\\cos t,\\sin t)$ on $C$, so the flux is $\\int_0^{2\\pi}(\\cos^2t+\\sin^2t)\\,dt=2\\pi$. Equivalently, rotate the field as in Problem 1(c) and use the corresponding circulation in Problem 2."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "92df2acd-bb90-5c44-81fa-525df536f59e"
  :answer/id #uuid "92df2acd-bb90-5c44-81fa-525df536f59e"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "For $r>0$, $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=1/r$. The formal area integral can be interpreted as an improper integral:\n\n$$\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\to0^+}\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_\\varepsilon^1\\frac1r\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\n=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\to0^+}2\\pi(1-\\varepsilon)=2\\pi.$$\n\nThe standard full-disk theorem does not apply directly because the field is undefined at the origin. The equality can be justified by applying Green's theorem to annuli and taking a limit."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
 {:db/id "04e2bfc2-14e3-57f6-9455-b8939e0b7a26"
