  :answer-field/correct "71c153c5-b242-591a-bc0e-031852d164f8"
  :db/ensure :answer-field/validate}
 {:db/id "9dbe21c8-f881-59e7-ad0d-69a6eb42dd99"
  :question/id #uuid "9dbe21c8-f881-59e7-ad0d-69a6eb42dd99"
  :question/problem "**(a)** $\\mathbf F=\\langle x,-y\\rangle$, $\\mathbf G=\\langle y,x\\rangle$ on $\\mathbb R^2$.\n\n(i) Confirm both are conservative. Use Problem 1 to show $\\mathbf F$ is an ideal flow (and so is $\\mathbf G$).\n\n(ii) Find potential functions for both fields. Use Problem 1 to derive a stream function for $\\mathbf F$."
  :question/answer-fields ["c479ba4b-591e-5cb9-a0e1-516fc2d62542"]
  :question/worked-solution "Differentiate each potential to check both components. For $\\psi=xy$, $\\psi_y=x=F_1$ and $\\psi_x=y=-F_2$. Additive constants do not change any of these fields. The calculations verify the worksheet's stated zero-divergence/zero-curl conditions."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "5bf6e3f3-eb49-5df5-bc3e-82d627dcb3d0"
  :answer/id #uuid "5bf6e3f3-eb49-5df5-bc3e-82d627dcb3d0"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "Take $\\phi_F=e^x\\cos y$ and $\\phi_G=e^x\\sin y$. Their gradients are exactly the two specified fields, so both are conservative. A stream function for $\\mathbf F$ is $\\psi=e^x\\sin y$.\n\nTheir divergences are $e^x\\cos y-e^x\\cos y=0$ and $e^x\\sin y-e^x\\sin y=0$. Their curls are also zero: for $\\mathbf F$, $-e^x\\sin y-(-e^x\\sin y)=0$; for $\\mathbf G$, $e^x\\cos y-e^x\\cos y=0$. Problem 1(b) gives $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F=\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf G=0$ and, after rotating $\\mathbf G$ to $-\\mathbf F$, $\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf G=-\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F=0$. Thus both meet the stated ideal-flow conditions."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
