 {:db/id "ed7bee5a-40a1-5e12-b77a-153dac1f8572"
  :question/id #uuid "ed7bee5a-40a1-5e12-b77a-153dac1f8572"
  :question/problem "**(a)** Prove, or otherwise convince yourself, that the line through $P$ with the same direction as $\\mathbf F(x,y)$ has slope $g(x,y)/f(x,y)$."
  :question/answer-fields ["7a5da541-73c6-5b07-965c-962d03521a2f"]
  :question/worked-solution "A direction vector $(f,g)$ has horizontal change proportional to $f$ and vertical change proportional to $g$. State the nonzero-denominator condition rather than assigning a finite slope to a vertical line."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "c34c8a72-6aec-5e68-bb83-22afb24b29ca"
  :answer/id #uuid "c34c8a72-6aec-5e68-bb83-22afb24b29ca"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "The printed $\\psi_t$ is a typo: the denominator must be $\\psi_y$. With $y=y(x)$, differentiate $\\psi(x,y(x))=c$:\n\n$$\\psi_x+\\psi_y\\frac{dy}{dx}=0.$$\n\nWhere $\\psi_y\\ne0$,\n\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{\\psi_x}{\\psi_y}.$$"
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
