 {:db/id "43a7a084-6042-5631-9a50-f49ed6051e5f"
  :question/id #uuid "43a7a084-6042-5631-9a50-f49ed6051e5f"
  :question/problem "**(b)** Use implicit differentiation of $\\psi(x,y)=c$ to show the displayed relation in the source:\n\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{\\psi_x}{\\psi_t}.$$\n\nThe denominator $\\psi_t$ is retained **as printed** in the PDF. Address the notation when deriving the relation."
  :question/answer-fields ["ba18b400-d617-5f64-a5df-06efa663ce90"]
  :question/worked-solution "Apply the chain rule to both dependencies of $\\psi$: its explicit $x$ argument and its $y(x)$ argument. No variable $t$ was introduced in the source context. Division by $\\psi_y$ requires $\\psi_y\\ne0$; other regular points can instead be described locally by $x$ as a function of $y$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "447c4af3-2141-5ebb-8aad-46e4298a2833"
  :answer/id #uuid "447c4af3-2141-5ebb-8aad-46e4298a2833"
  :answer/type :answer.type/text
  :answer/value "At a point with $f\\ne0$, substitute $\\psi_x=-g$ and $\\psi_y=f$ into the corrected relation from (b):\n\n$$\\frac{dy}{dx}=-\\frac{\\psi_x}{\\psi_y}=\\frac{g}{f}.$$\n\nThis is the slope of the field direction from (a), so the level curve is tangent to $\\mathbf F$.\n\nMore generally,\n\n$$\\nabla\\psi\\cdot\\mathbf F=(-g,f)\\cdot(f,g)=0.$$\n\nAt a regular point where $\\nabla\\psi\\ne0$, the gradient is normal to the level curve, so $\\mathbf F$ is tangent to it, including vertical tangents. Thus regular level curves follow the flow. At a stagnation point $\\mathbf F=0$, no nonzero field direction is specified; the zero vector still satisfies the orthogonality identity."
  :db/ensure :answer/validate}
