 {:db/id "31437968-eb0b-5e95-8502-b20a3264c863"
  :question/id #uuid "31437968-eb0b-5e95-8502-b20a3264c863"
  :question/problem "Source exercise: 3.1.12, printed page 128 (PDF page 140).\n\nIdentify the operation that changes the first matrix to the second, and explain its effect on the determinant:\n\n$$\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\c&d\\end{bmatrix}\\longrightarrow\\cdots\\longrightarrow\\begin{bmatrix}a&b\\\\2c&2d\\end{bmatrix}.$$"
  :question/answer-fields ["d4a99cdb-9cdc-5d48-a517-a369789cb6ee"]
  :question/worked-solution "Scaling one row by $2$ scales the determinant by $2$. Directly, $a(2d)-b(2c)=2(ad-bc)$. Only one row is scaled, so the factor is $2$, rather than $2^2$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
