 {:db/id "75171c23-6bf6-5fb3-b41d-ec62488dadcc"
  :question/id #uuid "75171c23-6bf6-5fb3-b41d-ec62488dadcc"
  :question/problem "Source exercise: 3.1.17, printed page 128 (PDF page 140).\n\nIs it true that $\\det(A+B)=\\det(A)+\\det(B)$? If true, explain why; if false, give a counterexample."
  :question/answer-fields ["edb7cfcf-8bea-5c68-b17b-63bc60867ac9"]
  :question/worked-solution "A single pair of square matrices for which the two sides differ disproves the assertion. Here $A+B=\\begin{bmatrix}2&0\\\\0&2\\end{bmatrix}$, whose determinant is $2\\cdot2=4$. The determinant is linear in one row at a time with the other rows fixed, not additive in the whole matrix."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
