 {:db/id "5b075aba-b77c-52f4-9246-23c2d050dae8"
  :question/id #uuid "5b075aba-b77c-52f4-9246-23c2d050dae8"
  :question/problem "Source exercise: 3.1.19, printed page 128 (PDF page 140).\n\nA real matrix is **orthogonal** if $A^T A=I$, so its inverse is its transpose. What are the possible values of $\\det(A)$?\n\nEnter the set of possible values: {{answer}}."
  :question/answer-fields ["702e7d4b-4c4d-50e8-8cd7-8bd6c18744bf"]
  :question/worked-solution "Taking determinants gives $\\det(A^T)\\det(A)=\\det(I)=1$. Since $\\det(A^T)=\\det(A)$, we have $(\\det A)^2=1$, hence $\\det A\\in\\{-1,1\\}$. Both occur: $I_n$ has determinant $1$, and $\\operatorname{diag}(-1,1,\\ldots,1)$ has determinant $-1$."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
