 {:db/id "33ae2fc2-38de-584c-a278-a991ff186cec"
  :question/id #uuid "33ae2fc2-38de-584c-a278-a991ff186cec"
  :question/problem "Source exercise: 3.1.20, printed page 129 (PDF page 141).\n\nLet $A,B$ be $n\\times n$ matrices. The notation $A\\sim B$ (**$A$ is similar to $B$**) means that some invertible matrix $P$ satisfies $A=P^{-1}BP$.\n\nShow that $A\\sim B$ implies $\\det(A)=\\det(B)$."
  :question/answer-fields ["c88b6705-f18d-5640-b40e-bc841c62e4d8"]
  :question/worked-solution "Use $\\det(XY)=\\det(X)\\det(Y)$ and $\\det(P^{-1})=1/\\det(P)$. Similarity uses the same invertible $P$ on the two sides of $B$ with opposite powers. The notation $\\sim$ here means similarity, as defined in this exercise."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
