 {:db/id "ae7af27d-2f3d-57f9-859d-20a413d678ca"
  :question/id #uuid "ae7af27d-2f3d-57f9-859d-20a413d678ca"
  :question/problem "Source exercise: 4.10.4, printed page 221 (PDF page 233).\n\nConsider\n\n$$\\vec v_1=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\1\\\\-2\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_2=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\-2\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_3=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\-3\\\\-2\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_4=\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\\\1\\\\2\\end{bmatrix}.$$\n\nIs $\\vec w=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\-1\\end{bmatrix}$ in their span? If so, exhibit a linear combination equal to $\\vec w$, using as few of the four vectors as possible."
  :question/answer-fields ["2520bb94-4364-510b-ad49-85cc3ab85797"]
  :question/worked-solution "If $\\sum c_j\\vec v_j=\\vec w$, the first-coordinate equation would give $c_1+c_2+c_3-c_4=1$, while the third would give $-2(c_1+c_2+c_3-c_4)=-1$. The first equation makes the third left side $-2$, a contradiction. The requested minimal combination is conditional on membership."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
 {:db/id "e8382d21-01cd-5028-957c-0d090d9ba1c0"
  :assigned-problem/id #uuid "e8382d21-01cd-5028-957c-0d090d9ba1c0"
