 {:db/id "7455514b-5312-5142-bf16-0346031c446d"
  :question/id #uuid "7455514b-5312-5142-bf16-0346031c446d"
  :question/problem "Source exercise: 4.10.11, printed page 222 (PDF page 234).\n\nAre the following vectors linearly independent? If they are, explain why. If they are not, exhibit one as a linear combination of the others. Also give a linearly independent set with the same span.\n\n$$\\vec v_1=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\3\\\\-1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_2=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\4\\\\-1\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_3=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\4\\\\0\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_4=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\10\\\\2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}.$$"
  :question/answer-fields ["6f11794d-626e-5b79-9985-1aeb29620714"]
  :question/worked-solution "The displayed combination checks in every coordinate: $-6+4+3=1$, $-18+16+12=10$, $6-4=2$, and $-6+4+3=1$. The first three rows of the matrix with columns $\\vec v_1,\\vec v_2,\\vec v_3$ form a $3\\times3$ minor with determinant $1$, so these three columns are independent. Removing $\\vec v_4$, which is generated by them, preserves the span."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
