 {:db/id "966de5ce-896c-5026-ae25-aa55dd1e00a5"
  :question/id #uuid "966de5ce-896c-5026-ae25-aa55dd1e00a5"
  :question/problem "Source exercise: 4.10.18, printed page 224 (PDF page 236).\n\nAre the following vectors linearly independent? If they are, explain why. If they are not, exhibit one as a linear combination of the others. Also give a linearly independent set with the same span.\n\n$$\\vec v_1=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\4\\\\-2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_2=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\5\\\\-3\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_3=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\7\\\\-5\\\\1\\end{bmatrix},\\quad\\vec v_4=\\begin{bmatrix}1\\\\5\\\\-2\\\\1\\end{bmatrix}.$$"
  :question/answer-fields ["cbbef084-1b60-5586-a0c5-aeba84ea1799"]
  :question/worked-solution "The combination checks as $-2(1,4,-2,1)+3(1,5,-3,1)=(1,7,-5,1)$. For the proposed independent set, use the first three coordinate rows. The determinant of $\\begin{bmatrix}1&1&1\\\\4&5&5\\\\-2&-3&-2\\end{bmatrix}$ is $1$, proving independence. The removed vector $\\vec v_3$ is generated by the retained ones, so their spans agree."
  :db/ensure :question/validate}
